# kiosk-linux/configs/qemu_x86_64_kiosk_defconfig
#
# A minimal (sparse) defconfig for Buildroot 2024.02.9 (the exact pinned version
# this was verified against — see build.sh's BUILDROOT_TAG) — NOT a full copy of
# stock configs/qemu_x86_64_defconfig with overrides layered on top. Only the
# symbols that differ from Buildroot's own defaults are listed below;
# `make defconfig` fills in everything else from Buildroot's standard defaults.
# Compared against the stock qemu_x86_64_defconfig's own settings, the intent
# per the task-1 brief was:
#   - BR2_TOOLCHAIN_BUILDROOT_CXX=n originally (no C++ needed for this base
#                                        image) — flipped to =y in sub-project
#                                        3 (Task 2), which needs C++ for WPE
#                                        WebKit; see that section below
#   - BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_HOSTNAME / _ISSUE (kiosk-linux branding)
#   - BR2_INIT_BUSYBOX=y                (explicit; BusyBox init is already the
#                                        Buildroot default, stated here for clarity)
#   - BR2_SYSTEM_DHCP=""                (no need for automatic eth0 DHCP on a
#                                        bare console-boot image; stock enables
#                                        it for its own networking tests)
#   - BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY                (adds our S99kiosk-boot-marker init script)
#   - BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO(+_GZIP)=y / BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2=n
#                                        (initramfs cpio.gz instead of stock's
#                                        ext2 image, per this task's interface
#                                        contract: output/images/rootfs.cpio.gz)
#   - BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="x86_64" instead of stock's
#                                        BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_USE_CUSTOM_CONFIG +
#                                        BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CUSTOM_CONFIG_FILE
#                                        pointing at Buildroot's own
#                                        board/qemu/x86_64/linux.config — using
#                                        the kernel's own in-tree defconfig
#                                        keeps this project independent of
#                                        Buildroot's qemu board directory
#                                        conventions, at the cost of losing a
#                                        few QEMU-specific kernel tweaks that
#                                        board/qemu/x86_64/linux.config carries
#                                        (none of which are required for a
#                                        plain serial-console boot-to-shell).
#
# BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_LINUX_HEADERS_CUSTOM_6_1 / BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CUSTOM_VERSION*
# / BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_NEEDS_HOST_LIBELF / BR2_PACKAGE_HOST_QEMU* are dropped:
# they exist in the stock file to pin an exact kernel version and build a
# host-qemu for Buildroot's own CI test suite (runtime-tests), neither of
# which this project needs — we bring our own QEMU (test/run-qemu.sh) and are
# fine with whatever kernel version BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="x86_64" pulls
# in via the Linux tarball Buildroot's package infrastructure selects for this
# release.

# Architecture
BR2_x86_64=y

# Toolchain
# BR2_TOOLCHAIN_BUILDROOT_CXX=n was sub-project 1's original base-image choice
# ("no C++ needed" — true for a boot-to-shell image). Sub-project 3 (Task 2)
# changes that: WPE WebKit hard-requires BR2_INSTALL_LIBSTDCPP (see
# package/wpewebkit/Config.in's "depends on BR2_INSTALL_LIBSTDCPP # harfbuzz,
# icu" and its "wpewebkit needs a toolchain w/ C++..." comment, confirmed by a
# real build attempt where the package silently disappeared from the .config
# with that exact unmet-dependency comment printed — not a guess). The
# internal Buildroot toolchain's C++ support is gated on this symbol, so it
# flips to =y here.
BR2_TOOLCHAIN_BUILDROOT_CXX=y

# System
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_HOSTNAME="kiosk-linux"
BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_ISSUE="RMPG Flex Kiosk Linux (sub-project 1: base image only)"
BR2_INIT_BUSYBOX=y
BR2_SYSTEM_DHCP=""

# Rootfs overlay. Buildroot resolves BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY relative to $(TOPDIR)
# (Buildroot's OWN source tree), never relative to O= — a relative path here
# is fragile for exactly that reason (this was tried first and failed once
# the build moved to a Docker named-volume O= output dir with a different
# relative depth than a plain host bind mount would have had: "rsync:
# change_dir .../buildroot/../../rootfs-overlay failed: No such file or
# directory"). Use the absolute in-container path instead — build.sh always
# bind-mounts this project's kiosk-linux/ directory to /kiosk-linux, so this
# path is stable regardless of where BUILDROOT_DIR or the O= volume live.
BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY="/kiosk-linux/rootfs-overlay"

# Filesystem: initramfs cpio.gz, not the stock ext2 image
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO=y
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIO_GZIP=y
BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2=n
# BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_TAR is not set

# Sub-project 5 (A/B boot partition): syslinux/extlinux is the bootloader
# that reads extlinux.conf off the new EXT2 boot partition assembled by
# kiosk-linux/scripts/assemble-disk-image.sh. Buildroot's own syslinux
# package is NOT used to generate extlinux.conf here (that config is
# hand-written per-slot by assemble-disk-image.sh) — this Kconfig option
# only ensures Buildroot's build tree has the pieces this project's own
# disk-assembly step needs available if it ever wants them; the actual
# extlinux/mbr.bin tooling used to assemble the disk image runs on the
# HOST-side Docker build container (see Dockerfile), not inside the target
# rootfs itself.
BR2_TARGET_SYSLINUX=y
BR2_TARGET_SYSLINUX_ISOLINUX=n

# Kernel: use the kernel's own in-tree x86_64 defconfig rather than
# Buildroot's board/qemu/x86_64/linux.config
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL=y
# LATEST_VERSION=y reads as a reproducibility smell at a glance, but it isn't
# one here: within a pinned Buildroot release (BUILDROOT_TAG), "latest" always
# resolves to the one specific kernel version that release's package
# infrastructure selects — a fresh build against the same BUILDROOT_TAG always
# pulls the same kernel.
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_LATEST_VERSION=y
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="x86_64"

# DRM/KMS graphics stack (sub-project 2, Task 1). The fragment merges into the
# kernel .config after BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="x86_64" is applied (see
# configs/kernel-drm.fragment for the merge-mechanism citation). Same absolute
# in-container path convention as BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY above, for the same reason:
# BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES was verified (linux/Config.in:194 in the
# real Buildroot checkout) to accept a plain path list passed straight to
# scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh with no $(TOPDIR)-relative resolution of its
# own, but build.sh only bind-mounts this project's kiosk-linux/ directory at
# /kiosk-linux inside the container (never at a path relative to BUILDROOT_DIR or
# the O= volume), so the absolute /kiosk-linux/... form is what's actually
# reachable at build time regardless of where Buildroot's source checkout lives.
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES="/kiosk-linux/configs/kernel-drm.fragment /kiosk-linux/configs/kernel-net.fragment /kiosk-linux/configs/kernel-storage.fragment"

# libdrm — DRM userspace library, plus its bundled test tools (provides modetest,
# the diagnostic tool this sub-project uses as its proof mechanism). Symbol names
# verified against the real Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout's
# package/libdrm/Config.in (both symbols matched the brief's best-effort names
# exactly — no correction needed here).
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBDRM=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBDRM_INSTALL_TESTS=y

# Mesa3D with the virgl/virtio-gpu Gallium driver — actual rendering capability,
# not just mode-setting. Verified against the real Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout's
# package/mesa3d/Config.in:257 — the real symbol is
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_GALLIUM_DRIVER_VIRGL (the brief's other guessed alternative,
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_DRIVER_VIRGL without "_GALLIUM_", does not exist in this
# release). Mesa3D's own Config.in already `select`s BR2_PACKAGE_LIBDRM, so the
# explicit BR2_PACKAGE_LIBDRM=y above is technically redundant once this is set,
# but is kept explicit since BR2_PACKAGE_LIBDRM_INSTALL_TESTS (modetest) is a
# sub-option of BR2_PACKAGE_LIBDRM that a `select` alone would not turn on.
BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D=y
BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_GALLIUM_DRIVER_VIRGL=y

# Added in sub-project 3 (browser). Plain -device virtio-gpu-pci under QEMU
# with -display none (headless, no host GUI/GL context) has no real GPU/GL
# acceleration — QEMU's virgl 3D backend needs an actual host OpenGL context,
# which a headless test harness cannot provide. Cog/WPE's DRM platform uses
# GBM+EGL for buffer allocation, which needs SOME working driver; without a
# software fallback, Mesa's GBM loader fails outright ("MESA-LOADER: failed
# to open kms_swrast" / "swrast", confirmed by a real run). This adds Mesa's
# software (LLVMpipe) Gallium driver as that fallback, verified against the
# real Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout's package/mesa3d/Config.in:215.
BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_GALLIUM_DRIVER_SWRAST=y

# Added after LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE/WEBKIT_DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE env-var
# workarounds both failed to fix a real, repeated boot: WPEWebProcess (the
# actual page-rendering sub-process) opens its own EGL context that tries
# Mesa's zink driver (OpenGL-over-Vulkan) and crash-loops when it can't load
# it. zink needs a working software Vulkan driver (lavapipe), which itself
# needs LLVM for shader JIT compilation — confirmed against the real
# Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout's package/mesa3d/Config.in:309
# (BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_VULKAN_DRIVER_SWRAST depends on
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_LLVM and already `select`s
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_GALLIUM_DRIVER_SWRAST above, so no double-declaration
# needed there).
BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_LLVM=y
BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_VULKAN_DRIVER_SWRAST=y

# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_GALLIUM_DRIVER_VIRGL's own `select` already turns on
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_OPENGL_EGL (which in turn selects HAS_LIBEGL_WAYLAND and
# MESA3D_GBM), but it does NOT select BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_OPENGL_ES — that's an
# independent, non-auto-selected option (mesa3d/Config.in:~365) that provides
# BR2_PACKAGE_HAS_LIBGLES. WPE WebKit's Config.in has a hard `depends on
# BR2_PACKAGE_HAS_LIBGLES # libepoxy`, discovered the same way as the C++
# requirement above (a real build attempt printed wpewebkit's "needs an
# OpenGL ES w/ EGL-capable Wayland backend" unmet-dependency comment with this
# symbol missing) — so it must be turned on explicitly here, sub-project 2's
# defconfig having only needed the Gallium/EGL/GBM path for modetest, not GLES.
BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_OPENGL_ES=y

# Networking (sub-project 3, Task 1). udhcpc (BusyBox) acquires a DHCP lease
# over the virtio-net interface; ca-certificates provides the Mozilla trust
# root bundle WPE WebKit needs to validate rmpgutah.us's TLS certificate.
BR2_PACKAGE_BUSYBOX_CONFIG_FRAGMENT_FILES="/kiosk-linux/configs/busybox-net.fragment"
BR2_PACKAGE_CA_CERTIFICATES=y

# Kiosk browser (sub-project 3, Task 2). WPE WebKit is the embedded-Linux
# WebKit port purpose-built for kiosk/DRM use (chosen over Chromium — Buildroot
# has no Chromium package, and building it from source would need Google's own
# depot_tools/gn/ninja toolchain plus a 30-100GB source tree, realistically its
# own separate multi-day program). Cog is the minimal fullscreen launcher shell
# around it, rendering directly against the DRM/KMS backend from sub-project 2
# with no X11/Wayland compositor needed. Exact symbol names verified against
# the pinned Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout per Task 2 Step 1: BR2_PACKAGE_WPEWEBKIT
# and BR2_PACKAGE_COG matched the brief's guessed names exactly, but the DRM
# platform backend needed an additional sub-option not in the brief's snippet —
# BR2_PACKAGE_COG_PLATFORM_DRM defaults to unset (only the Wayland/FDO backend
# defaults to y in cog/Config.in), so it must be explicitly turned on for
# `cog --platform=drm` to be a valid choice at runtime. Its own depends
# (BR2_PACKAGE_HAS_LIBEGL / _HAS_LIBGBM / LIBGBM_HAS_FEATURE_DMA_BUF /
# LIBGBM_HAS_FEATURE_FORMAT_MODIFIER_PLANE_COUNT) are already satisfied by the
# Mesa3D virgl Gallium driver enabled above in sub-project 2.
BR2_PACKAGE_WPEWEBKIT=y
BR2_PACKAGE_COG=y
BR2_PACKAGE_COG_PLATFORM_DRM=y

# BR2_PACKAGE_COG_PLATFORM_DRM's own `depends on BR2_PACKAGE_HAS_UDEV # libinput`
# is unmet by this image's defaults — a first real build attempt confirmed
# BR2_PACKAGE_COG_PLATFORM_DRM silently dropped out of .config with nothing
# else providing BR2_PACKAGE_HAS_UDEV. That virtual symbol only comes from
# eudev (package/eudev/Config.in) or systemd; eudev is the minimal choice here
# (systemd would be a far larger, unrelated addition). eudev itself further
# depends on BR2_ROOTFS_DEVICE_CREATION_DYNAMIC_EUDEV — one arm of Buildroot's
# System configuration > /dev management choice (mutually exclusive with the
# devtmpfs-only / static / mdev alternatives) — which must be selected as the
# active device-creation policy for eudev to even appear as choosable.
BR2_ROOTFS_DEVICE_CREATION_DYNAMIC_EUDEV=y
BR2_PACKAGE_EUDEV=y

# Debugging aids (temporary, sub-project 3 troubleshooting). WPEWebProcess
# crashes with zero diagnostic output anywhere in cog.log or the kernel
# serial log (no segfault/OOM message either) once EGL genuinely initializes
# — strace and an on-target gdb are needed to get a real backtrace/syscall
# trace at the crash point. Symbols verified against the real Buildroot
# 2024.02.9 checkout's package/gdb/Config.in and package/strace/Config.in.
BR2_PACKAGE_GDB=y
BR2_PACKAGE_GDB_DEBUGGER=y
BR2_PACKAGE_STRACE=y

# Real root cause found via strace: fontconfig scans every standard font
# directory (/usr/share/fonts, /usr/local/share/fonts, /.fonts,
# /.local/share/fonts) and gets ENOENT on all of them — this image never
# installed any font at all — then WPEWebProcess hits a fatal GLib
# assertion and self-aborts (tgkill SIGABRT) immediately after finishing
# that scan. WebKit requires at least one available font to initialize
# its text layout engine. DejaVu is Buildroot's standard minimal font
# package for exactly this need; symbol verified against the real
# Buildroot 2024.02.9 checkout's package/dejavu/Config.in.
BR2_PACKAGE_DEJAVU=y

# Debugging aid: the SIGSEGV crash inside libcogplatform-drm.so was only
# reachable via unsymbolized ("??") gdb frames on the stripped target
# binary — a manual attempt to swap in the unstripped intermediate build
# artifact directly (bypassing Buildroot fakeroot/mkusers/makedevs
# packaging) broke boot outright (missing device nodes -> kernel panic).
# BR2_STRIP_EXCLUDE_FILES is the correct, minimal-footprint way to keep
# exactly this one file unstripped through the real, correct packaging
# pipeline, verified against the real Buildroot Config.in.
BR2_STRIP_EXCLUDE_FILES="libcogplatform-drm.so"

# REVERTED: BR2_ENABLE_RUNTIME_DEBUG + an explicit -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
# override (see kiosk-linux/build.sh) were tried to get WebKit own internal
# RELEASE_LOG/debug logging compiled in, after raw pixel-byte inspection
# proved the blank-render bug lives inside WebKit own compositor. Confirmed
# via a real, full rebuild (~50+ minutes) that this WebKit
# version/Buildroot combination does NOT support Debug mode cleanly — the
# build reached 99.9% completion and then failed to LINK
# ("undefined reference to JSC::UnlinkedMetadataTable::~UnlinkedMetadataTable()"),
# a genuine structural incompatibility, not a quick patch. Left disabled;
# see build.sh own comment on the -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug patch for the
# full history if attempting this again.
# BR2_ENABLE_RUNTIME_DEBUG=y
